A||N||7||Will||Front lower control arm bushing replacement||||1||
Z||000000||Will||05-18-2001||06:23 PM||||I recently made aset of delrin bushings for my car. I installed the front uppers without major incident last weekend, and am now installing the front lowers.<BR>Is there a magic way to get the control arm bolts out without removing the spring? Should I just play with the jack until the tension is off of them?<P>The left rear bolt is seized to the inner sleeve of the stock bushing, and broke loose from the rubber as I removed the nut. Is there a trick for this or should I just go at it with a BFH?||||reg||1
Z||000001||DRH||05-18-2001||06:38 PM||||Try to use a punch or small chisel and hit the seam on the sleeve on each end. It's kinda hard to get it in there but will loosen it up some if you can get a good hit or two on it.<P>I got that tip from Ogre's cave. It helped on all but one of mine. Actually it may have helped on that one too but I still wound up beating the bolt out.<P>Penetrating oil doesn't hurt either although I'm not sure how much it helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bob Englert||05-18-2001||09:25 PM||||On my first Fiero I had to cut out 2 of the lower control arm bolts with a sawzall.  What a pain!!!<P>I am just now taking the suspension apart on a Michigan bred 87 GT.  There is a lot of surface rust and I am not looking forward to the lower control arm bolts.  Hopefully I won't have to buy more packages of saw blades.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||05-18-2001||10:46 PM||||What type of sawzall blade did you use Bob?  I tried a couple different blades, but I couldn't even cut into the sleeve, just wanted to make it shiny.  Hacksaw didn't work either.<P>I used a 4" cutoff wheel, carefully cut the sleeve and bolt.  Made pretty sparks.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||05-18-2001||11:00 PM||||Baning on the stuck bolt just spread the bracket. I was able to cut the bolt, but there seems to be some kind of hard oxidized coating on the inner sleeves (I tried sawing on the inner sleeve from one of the upper bushings--that sleeve came out by hand), although once you get through the coating it can be cut. I'm going to use the die grinder tomorrow.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bob Englert||05-19-2001||07:51 AM||||I used a package of medium teeth blades from Sears.  I don't remember exactly the type.  I might get a cut off wheel and grinder next time.  Sounds easier.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000006||Pontiaddict||05-19-2001||08:26 PM||||I had to use an air hammer on mine to get them free from the sleeves. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||05-19-2001||10:58 PM||||I finished the left side. I ended up drilling the head off the bolt. Did you know that the pivots for the lower control arm are parallel, but not concentric?!? WTF?!? What kind of baboon desgined this car?<P>I'm leaving the right side till next weekend, since the left side was so much fun. The rear bolt is seized on that side, too.||||reg||1
